 kind of  one  one takeaway or one probe wisdom to  share with folks who are looking to  compete in this creative arts  competition  or entrepreneurship competitions more  broadly uh what would that kind of  key takeaway or probably jumpy yeah i'm  uh  i'm gonna sound like a broken record but  it's really your ability to communicate  is the most important thing you can have  the coolest idea in the world the  coolest product but if you cannot  communicate that to others and they  you can't get people excited about it or  ultimately buy your product  it doesn't matter like there's so many  cool technologies that never got off the  ground  or cool technologies that you know where  the inventor didn't end up getting  credit because they weren't the ones to  commercialize it right like the um  the mp3 being like one of the best  examples in history  um and and so you know not only should  you  focus on communication and it shouldn't  be a second thought it really should be  one of your primary  focuses just like you know technology  and product development is for so many  of us at mit  um you know practicing communication is  just  the most important thing um and not only  for this competition that i know  but um but really in the long run and  and figuring out  not only what you're going to say but  how you're going to deliver it um  equally important  i have a question sure um  would we what would be your opinion  i mean um is there any an important  difference between  our focused businesses and  other type of businesses such as  technology focus ones  and the other question would be  uh what would be the key challenges for  for art projects and how can we  [ __ ] uh can we  face them yeah that's a great question  so so the first thing i will say is that  i think um  creative arts businesses can be  technology  um businesses so so they can be two  different things but they  you know i think for the purpose  especially this competition they they're  you know one in th